Protect a newly constructed byte-code assignment call while signaling and
formatting an error.

When a byte-compiled assignment raises an error, getCurrentCall() can
inflate its assignment expression into a fresh LANGSXP. errorcall() passed
that call through vsignalError() and verrorcall_dflt() without protecting it.
A collection before deparse1s() could reclaim the call, producing an
"unprotected object ... (was LANGSXP)" abort under the strict barrier.

Found by OSS-Fuzz testcase 6567538138611712 (libFuzzer_r_eval) with input
`x <- list(); x; x[2] <- x$a*9999`.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #416, filed 2026-10-28.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk.

diff --git a/src/main/errors.c b/src/main/errors.c
index 5c88cc52d8..6e2aa30fb3 100644
--- a/src/main/errors.c
+++ b/src/main/errors.c
@@ -920,2 +920,5 @@ NORET void errorcall(SEXP call, const char *format,...)
 
+    /* getCurrentCall() can construct a new byte-code assignment call. */
+    PROTECT(call);
+
     va_start(ap, format);
     vsignalError(call, format, ap);
     va_end(ap);
